    "reply": "## Lynch Syndrome Condition Record\n\nLynch syndrome is recorded as a disease concept in the knowledge graph under MedGen [7][19]. The condition is also recognized in the literature under the historical designation Hereditary Nonpolyposis Colorectal Cancer [23] and has been subclassified into site-specific colonic cancer (Lynch syndrome I) [20] and Lynch Syndrome II [26]. A PubMed entry titled \"Lynch syndrome\" provides a general reference overview of the condition [18].\n\n## Causal Genes\n\nThe mismatch repair (MMR) pathway genes are the core causal candidates for Lynch syndrome. MutL homolog 1 (NCBIGene:4292) and mutS homolog 2 (NCBIGene:4436) are the two most frequently implicated MMR genes [10][11]. MutS homolog 6 (NCBIGene:2956) and PMS1 homolog 2, mismatch repair system component (NCBIGene:5395) are additional MMR genes associated with the condition [8][14]. PMS1 homolog 1, mismatch repair system component (NCBIGene:5378) and mutL homolog 3 (NCBIGene:27030) are also listed among genes linked to Lynch syndrome [13][6]. Transforming growth factor beta receptor 2 (NCBIGene:7048) is included as a gene associated with the condition [16].\n\n## Additional Associated Genes\n\nSeveral genes outside the core MMR pathway appear in the Lynch syndrome association set. Epithelial cell adhesion molecule (NCBIGene:4072) and cadherin 1 (NCBIGene:999) are listed among the associated genes [9][17]. Neurofibromin 1 (NCBIGene:4763) and ribosomal protein S20 (NCBIGene:6224) are also included in the retrieved gene set [12][15].\n\n## Current Clinical Trials\n\nFive clinical trials related to Lynch syndrome were retrieved. One trial focuses on implementing a new strategy to identify HNPCC patients [1]. Another study examines hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer in Taiwan through genetic and clinical approaches [2]. A third trial investigates psychosocial aspects of genetic testing for hereditary nonpolyposis colon cancer [3]. An educational CD-ROM intervention for informed consent in colorectal cancer patients or those with a family history is being compared with standard care [4]. A registry trial is enrolling women at risk for or diagnosed with Lynch syndrome [5].",
